  data:  # d989
    kind: invariant  # d989
    title: "META: Derived artifacts and dispatched work must declare a lifecycle policy"  # d989
    status: violated  # d989
    priority: 2  # d989
    parent:  # d989
    tags:  # d989
    - analysis_quality  # d989
    - lifecycle  # d989
    - hygiene  # d989
    isbefore: []  # d989
    duplicate_of: []  # d989
    not_duplicate_of: []  # d989
    pr_ref:  # d989
    description: "## Statement (also in `fields.statement`)\n\nWhen hypergumbo creates a derived artifact (cache entry, `/tmp` directory, intermediate output) OR dispatches a unit of analysis work (analyzer pass, linker run, fan-out task), it must either:\n\n- (a) operate within a **declared lifecycle policy** — quota, eviction rule, expiration, pre-filter, short-circuit when no input is present — OR\n- (b) be a one-shot operation whose lifecycle is **explicitly documented as ephemeral / hand-managed** (e.g., \"user is expected to clean `/tmp` between runs\").\n\nProducers that create artifacts indefinitely with no policy AND no documentation are violating this invariant. Dispatched work that runs without checking whether there's any input to process is also violating.\n\n## Root cause (also in `fields.root_cause`)\n\nNo project-wide convention exists for \"what's the lifecycle policy for this artifact / this dispatched unit?\". Each producer chose its own implicit policy, usually \"no policy\". The members of this META are independent producers each silently growing without bound or wasting compute on empty inputs.\n\nThe fix is twofold: (1) declare a small menu of lifecycle policies that producers must choose from (e.g., LRU quota, age-based eviction, file-presence pre-filter, ephemeral), and (2) audit existing producers and assign each one. Single-item fixes (just evict from cache X) treat symptoms; the META framing forces the menu-and-discipline conversation.\n\n## Members\n\n- `INV-padum` (P2 violated) — Hypergumbo's analysis cache at `~/.cache/hypergumbo/` grows without bounds (observed: 7.9GB / 76 entries / 1 repo / 3 days). No auto-eviction. **Design direction (user-set 2026-05-17): err on the side of retaining; do NOT auto-evict.** Instead, emit a loud reminder (\"honk\") when the cache exceeds a threshold (default 1 GB) and provide a config knob for the user to raise or lower it. The lifecycle policy here is \"retain-with-warning, threshold configurable\" rather than \"evict\".\n- `WI-jupar` (P2 todo_hard) — `/tmp/hypergumbo_sketch_compare/` accumulates indefinitely across all repos in a shared directory (27 files / ~30MB observed since Apr 21). The \"temporarily\" wording in the producer's docstring is aspirational; nothing actually cleans up. Candidate lifecycle: ephemeral-per-process (cleanup on exit) OR documented hand-managed.\n- `WI-jadig` (P2 todo_hard) — 113 of 133 AnalysisRuns on hypergumbo self-analysis processed 0 files but consumed 12.7s wall-clock (13.3% of total analyze time). Pass dispatch happens without checking whether any file matches the pass's language. Candidate lifecycle: file-presence pre-filter before dispatch.\n- `INV-bovif` (P2 violated, **primary parent is schema-spec drift META**) — Secondary symptom of lifecycle gap: 4 framework linkers run on 310-817 files and produce 0 edges, wasting ~10s of compute per run. **Not parented here** (root cause is schema migration, not lifecycle). Listed for cross-reference because the wasted-compute shape matches.\n\n## Why this is an invariant, not three independent fixes\n\nEach member's fix is producer-specific (different cache, different /tmp dir, different dispatcher). But the underlying question — *what lifecycle policy does this producer follow?* — is uniform. Without a shared menu of policies, every new producer reinvents the question and usually answers \"none\". Filing INV-padum's eviction logic doesn't prevent the next cache-like artifact from accumulating; filing WI-jupar's cleanup doesn't prevent the next `/tmp` dir.\n\nThe META forces the question to be answered at the producer level: *when you create this artifact or dispatch this work, what policy governs its lifecycle?*\n\n## The honk pattern (per user direction, 2026-05-17)\n\nFor artifacts where retention is more valuable than eviction (caches, archives, historical data), the lifecycle policy is:\n\n- **Honk threshold** — when size exceeds a default (e.g., 1 GB for HG cache), emit a loud user-visible reminder.\n- **User-configurable** — threshold is a config knob the user can raise (to silence reminders if disk is plentiful) or lower (to honk earlier if disk is constrained).\n- **No automatic eviction** — the artifact is preserved; the user decides when to clean up.\n\nThis is one valid policy in the menu. Other valid policies (eviction-based, pre-filter-based, ephemeral) apply to other artifact types. The discipline is **declare which policy, with what parameters**; the discipline is NOT \"always evict\".\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nThe META closes `satisfied` when:\n\n1. A small documented menu of lifecycle policies exists (e.g., in `docs/` or as an ADR).\n2. Each existing producer of derived artifacts / dispatched work declares its policy (in a docstring or registry).\n3. Each member item closes via the appropriate policy for its case (INV-padum: honk-threshold; WI-jupar: ephemeral-per-process or documented hand-managed; WI-jadig: file-presence pre-filter).\n\n## Related but separate\n\n- **Schema-spec drift META**: covers contract-declared-but-unimplemented. This META covers implementation-without-lifecycle. INV-bovif is primarily a member of schema-spec drift (its root cause is migration regression) but its wasted-compute symptom maps to this META — listed for cross-reference, not parented here.\n- **WI-ribag** (P2 todo_hard) — sketch slow on warm cache. Lifecycle-adjacent (it's about cache hit behavior) but the bug is recomputation-on-hit, not lifecycle-of-artifact. Not a member."  # d989
    fields:  # d989
      statement: "When hypergumbo creates a derived artifact (cache entry, /tmp directory, intermediate output) OR dispatches a unit of analysis work (analyzer pass, linker run, fan-out task), it must either: (a) operate within a declared lifecycle policy — quota, eviction rule, expiration, pre-filter, short-circuit when no input is present — OR (b) be a one-shot operation whose lifecycle is explicitly documented as ephemeral / hand-managed. Producers that create artifacts indefinitely with no policy AND no documentation are violating this invariant. Dispatched work that runs without checking whether there's any input to process is also violating."  # d989
      root_cause: "No project-wide convention exists for 'what's the lifecycle policy for this artifact / this dispatched unit?'. Each producer chose its own implicit policy, usually 'no policy'. Members are independent producers each silently growing without bound or wasting compute on empty inputs. The fix is twofold: (1) declare a small menu of lifecycle policies that producers must choose from (LRU quota, age-based eviction, file-presence pre-filter, honk-threshold-with-retention, ephemeral), and (2) audit existing producers and assign each one. Single-item fixes treat symptoms; the META framing forces the menu-and-discipline conversation."  # d989
